## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: ac494c6c2710a207f51a219e3b09360a9c94203c

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I used `git log -p -L 570,573:<file>` at the fix commit to trace the exact line's history backward through every revision. This directly shows the sequence of changes to that specific line/statement over time, rather than guessing via blame or searching commit messages.

**(2) Specific change that introduced the bug:** Commit `ac494c6c2710a207f51a219e3b09360a9c94203c` ("more diagnostics for EA-68014") refactored the previous if/else block:
```java
if (message.startsWith("<html>")) {
  tooltip = XmlStringUtil.wrapInHtml(XmlStringUtil.stripHtml(message) + link);
}
else {
  tooltip = XmlStringUtil.wrapInHtml(XmlStringUtil.escapeString(message) + link);
}
```
into a single ternary expression, but placed the closing parenthesis of `wrapInHtml(...)` incorrectly — right after the ternary condition instead of after `+ link`:
```java
tooltip = XmlStringUtil.wrapInHtml(message.startsWith("<html>") ? XmlStringUtil.stripHtml(message): XmlStringUtil.escapeString(message)) + link ;
```
This changes program semantics: `link` (the "show inspection description" HTML anchor) is now appended *outside* the `<html>...</html>` wrapper produced by `wrapInHtml`, rather than being included *inside* the HTML body as before. This malformed the tooltip HTML, which is documented in JetBrains issue IDEA-140967 as "Not all warnings are displayed in the editor" — the malformed HTML tooltip apparently interfered with the highlighting/rendering pipeline, causing some warnings not to display.

**(3) Evidence:** The bug-fixing commit's diff is exactly the inverse of this refactor's parenthesis-placement mistake — it moves the closing paren back to wrap `+ link` inside `wrapInHtml(...)`, restoring the original (pre-refactor) semantics exactly. No functional changes since `ac494c6c2710a207f51a219e3b09360a9c94203c` touched this line before the fix, so this is the sole introducing commit. I have high confidence in this identification since the fix is a pure syntactic reversal of the refactor's error on the same line.

